    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-29834: integrating 1/x gives ln|x|; the selected form absorbs the arbitrary constant using K>0. q-104297: the product rule gives e^x(cos x−sin x). q-48516: differentiating gives e^x(cos x+sin x), which equals −e^π at x=π. q-70667: cos 0=1, cos(−π)=−1, and the range is [−1,1], so only I and II hold. q-27009: cos(π/2)=0, cos(2π)=1 is a maximum, and cos 0=1, so only I holds. q-70672 and q-103242 use cosine's period 2π. q-158059 additionally uses cosine's evenness, giving π·0−1/2=−1/2. q-136629 uses evenness directly. q-31245 and q-71900 use the constant-factor rule and the antiderivative of e^x. q-113887 and q-66139 use the antiderivative a^x/ln a. q-2277 and q-31252 integrate term by term, with the latter also using the power rule. q-47998: the quotient rule gives (x² cos x−2x sin x)/x⁴=(x cos x−2 sin x)/x³ for x≠0. Confirmation rests on captured worked solutions; no grading evidence was supplied.",
